From 1b589e089023f507478c38c482c9b80445e80519 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mehdashti <mehdashti@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:07:36 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] genesys: send full scale as the last gamma table entry

sanei_genesys_send_gamma_table() forced the last entry of each gamma table to 0.
That entry is the output for the top of the input range, so every pixel whose
channel reached full scale came out black in that channel. On a Canon LiDE 100
this produced yellow vertical stripes (blue at full scale) and red, yellow and
magenta speckles along bright edges such as the rim of a plastic card. 16-bit
scans were unaffected because they bypass the gamma table.

Send 0xffff instead, as the old FIXME already suspected.
---
 backend/genesys/low.cpp | 10 +++++-----
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/backend/genesys/low.cpp b/backend/genesys/low.cpp
index 3ffc24a..7906351 100644
--- a/backend/genesys/low.cpp
+++ b/backend/genesys/low.cpp
@@ -741,11 +741,11 @@ void sanei_genesys_send_gamma_table(Genesys_Device* dev, const Genesys_Sensor& s
       val &= ~(0x01 << i);
         dev->interface->write_register(0xbe, val);
 
-      // FIXME: currently the last word of each gamma table is not initialized, so to work around
-      // unstable data, just set it to 0 which is the most likely value of uninitialized memory
-      // (proper value is probably 0xff)
-      gamma[size * 2 * i + size * 2 - 2] = 0;
-      gamma[size * 2 * i + size * 2 - 1] = 0;
+      // The last word is the output for the top of the input range. Setting it to 0 turns any
+      // channel that reaches full scale black: on the Canon LiDE 100 that showed up as yellow
+      // vertical stripes and as red/yellow/magenta speckles along bright edges. Use full scale.
+      gamma[size * 2 * i + size * 2 - 2] = 0xff;
+      gamma[size * 2 * i + size * 2 - 1] = 0xff;
 
       /* set GMM_Z */
         dev->interface->write_register(0xc5+2*i, gamma[size*2*i+1]);
